# arvo-bits

An exact-width bit container. Not a numeral, not a format, no arithmetic.

`Bits<const N: u32>` holds a value known to be `N` bits wide, from one bit to sixty-four. Construction
masks whatever you hand it down to the low `N` bits rather than rejecting anything, because there is
nothing to reject: a bit pattern of the wrong width is not an invalid value, it is a value with some
bits you asked to be dropped. Casting to a different width is the same masking arithmetic at a
different bound, so widening never loses a bit and narrowing loses exactly the ones above the new
width, on purpose.

This is the shape a consumer wants when a value's width is pinned by something arvo does not own, a
disk format, a wire format, a hash function's output size, and the consumer needs to name that exact
width, alias it under their own type, and convert it to whatever wider or narrower width their
boundary demands, on their own terms. Nothing here validates external bytes and nothing here decides
when a consumer's conversion happens; both stay the consumer's.

Zero workspace dependencies. `no_std`, no allocation, sizes const.

